artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a social organization modeled as a system whose state evolves over time and which may be subjected to exogenous or endogenous disruptions ("crises"). Suppose the organization possesses a set of core capabilities, quantified by some measurable performance indicators, and that its resilience is characterized by the following three factors:

(i) Vulnerability: the susceptibility of the organization's state and capabilities to disruptions;
(ii) Anticipation: the organization's level of foresight or situational awareness regarding potential vulnerabilities and incoming disruptions;
(iii) Adaptive capacity: the organization's flexibility or fluidity in mitigating vulnerabilities and responding to disruptions.

Develop a formal mathematical or computational model of such an organization in which these three factors enter as identifiable parameters or mechanisms, and use it to determine, in a precise and predictive way, why and how some organizations not only survive a crisis but emerge with enhanced core capabilities, while others fail. In particular, characterize the conditions on vulnerability, anticipation, and adaptive capacity (and their interactions) that separate the regime of post-crisis improvement from the regime of collapse.

